TP.register({id:"lp7-g3",lp:7,grade:"3",mode:"curriculum",checks:6,nextGrade:"4th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FLU_TITLE="The Thursday Crew";const FLU=["The Westmill garden sits behind the library, on a lot that used to be gravel. Twelve raised beds run in two long rows. A water barrel stands at each end.","Every Thursday after school, a crew of six shows up to work. They pull weeds, fill the watering cans, and check the beans for bugs. Nobody is in charge for long. The job list hangs on the gate. Whoever gets there first picks a job and crosses it off.","Luz likes the watering best. She fills two cans, walks the row, and counts to eight at every plant so each one gets the same drink.","Nobody taught them to count to eight. Luz started it her first week because she could not tell when a plant had enough. By the end of the month the whole crew was counting. Now the newest person on the crew learns it from whoever has been there longest. Nobody remembers that it was Luz’s idea.","The job list is the only rule, and even the list bends. If somebody shows up tired, the crew gives them the shade and the water bottle and does the row without them. Nobody writes that down either. It is just what the Thursday crew does, and it is the reason six people keep coming back to a lot that used to be gravel.","When the list is done, the crew sits on the low wall and splits a bag of carrots from the share shelf. The carrots are small and a little crooked. They taste better outside."].join("\n\n");const STORY_TITLE="The Row Nobody Planted";const STORY=["Bed seven at the Westmill garden stayed empty all spring. It belonged to Mr. Okafor, who had broken his wrist in March and could not dig.","Dalia noticed it first. There are twelve beds at Westmill, and when she counted the ones with something growing in them she always got eleven.","On the last Thursday in April, she brought it up at the gate. The crew talked for a while. Then they got the seed box down and planted bed seven with lettuce, radishes, and two rows of beans, and they did not tell anyone.","Mr. Okafor came back in June with his wrist out of the cast. He stood at bed seven for a long minute. Then he picked one radish, held it up, and asked the crew who to thank.","Nobody answered. Dalia looked at her shoes and smiled."].join("\n\n");const TABLE_TITLE="The Long Table";const TABLE=["Every August, Westmill carries its tables out to the garden path and sets them end to end. By noon the line of tables is longer than the garden itself, and people call it the long table.","Mina's kitchen was a beehive all morning. Her uncle chopped, her cousin stirred, and somebody kept moving the salt.","“Carry it slow,” her uncle said. “That pot is heavier than it looks.”","When they set their pot down, Mr. Haddad was already there with bread. He is the kind of neighbor who would give you the shirt off his back.","Mina's little brother was in charge of the napkins. He took the job to heart and counted them twice.","The news that there was still soup left traveled like wildfire down the table. Mina sat on the end with a warm bowl in her hands, and the whole afternoon flew by."].join("\n\n");const SHELF_TITLE="The Share Shelf";const SHELF=["Behind the Westmill garden gate stands a wooden cabinet with four shelves. The town calls it the share shelf.","The rule is painted on the door: take what you need, leave the rest. Anyone may open it. Nobody signs anything. The shelf is open from seven in the morning until dark.","In one week last August, the crew weighed everything that came in. The total was 212 pounds of food, mostly tomatoes, squash, and beans.","The shelf started with one person. In March, a neighbor left a paper bag of extra seed packets on the garden wall with a note that said help yourself. By the end of that week there were three bags on the wall. Then it rained for five days and the bags fell apart. The crew built the cabinet in April.","About 38 families use the shelf now. The crew still weighs the food once a year, in August, to see how the shelf is doing."].join("\n\n");const PLAN_READ=["How the Saturday Crew Gets Ready.","The Westmill garden runs a work morning every Saturday from April to October. The crew gets ready the night before so that nobody has to stand around waiting.","On Friday, two people write the job list and hang it on the gate. One person fills the water barrels. One person counts the trowels in the shed, because more people come than sign up.","By eight on Saturday morning, the tools are out and the list is up. New helpers pick a job off the list, so nobody has to ask what to do. The whole morning takes about two hours."].join("\n\n");const WORDS=["kindness","teamwork","sunflower","replant","unpack","mislabel","thankful","spotless","helpful","invitation","neighbor","wheelbarrow"];const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const pills=(...w)=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap">${w.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.4rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at ${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const group=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const stackFig=inner=>`<div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px;align-items:center;margin-top:10px">${inner}</div>`;const shelfHtml=small=>F.passage(SHELF,SHELF_TITLE,!!small);function noteBoxes(a,b){const bx=t=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;min-width:250px;min-height:100px;padding:8px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0;font-size:1.35rem">${t}</p></div>`;return`<div class="figure">${bx(a)}${bx(b)}</div>`}function stripRect(ws,alt){const S=220,T=ws.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${S+8} ${S+8}" width="${S+8}" role="img" aria-label="${alt}">`,x=0;ws.forEach(w=>{s+=`<rect x="${(4+x/T*S).toFixed(2)}" y="4" width="${(w/T*S).toFixed(2)}" height="${S}" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3"/>`;x+=w});return s+"</svg>"}const PANTRY_TITLE="The Bus Stop Pantry";const PANTRY=["Two towns over from Westmill, a different shelf stands at a bus stop on Grove Road. It is a metal box with a glass door, bolted to a post.","It holds soup cans, rice, and pasta instead of fresh food, because nothing has to be picked or weighed. A painted sign says take what you need. The town library keeps it filled, and there is a small light inside, so the box still works after dark. Nobody remembers who put the first can in it."].join("\n\n");const pantryHtml=small=>F.passage(PANTRY,PANTRY_TITLE,!!small);const HEIGHTS=[2,2.5,2.5,3,3.25,3.25,3.25,3.5];function dotPlot(){const w=660,h=272,x0=70,x1=600,n=8,step=(x1-x0)/n,ax=186;const X=v=>x0+(v-2)/.25*step;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${w} ${h}" width="620" role="img" aria-label="A line plot of 8 seedling heights in inches. One dot above 2, two dots above 2 and a half, one dot above 3, three dots above 3 and a quarter, and one dot above 3 and a half.">`;s+=`<line x1="${x0-20}" y1="${ax}" x2="${x1+20}" y2="${ax}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;const lbl={2:"2","2.5":"2 1/2",3:"3","3.5":"3 1/2",4:"4"};for(let i=0;i<=n;i++){const v=2+i*.25,x=X(v),big=lbl[String(v)]!==void 0;s+=`<line x1="${x}" y1="${ax}" x2="${x}" y2="${ax+(big?14:8)}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="${big?2.5:1.5}"/>`;if(big)s+=`<text x="${x}" y="${ax+36}" font-size="19" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${lbl[String(v)]}</text>`}const seen={};HEIGHTS.forEach(v=>{seen[v]=(seen[v]||0)+1;s+=`<circle cx="${X(v)}" cy="${ax-16-(seen[v]-1)*26}" r="9" fill="var(--sea)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`});s+=`<text x="${(x0+x1)/2}" y="${ax+64}" font-size="20" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--muted)">Seedling height in inches</text>`;return s+"</svg>"}const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Gate Sign Words",short:"Gate Words",parts:[{std:["RF.3.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:75,dir:"Read each word out loud. Look for a part you already know at the start or the end of the word.",desc:"reading longer words with prefixes, suffixes, and more than one syllable",key:["Read each word in order: kindness, teamwork, sunflower, replant, unpack, mislabel, thankful, spotless, helpful, invitation, neighbor, wheelbarrow.","Watch for: compound words (teamwork, sunflower, wheelbarrow), prefixes re-, un-, mis- (replant, unpack, mislabel), suffixes -ness, -ful, -less (kindness, thankful, helpful, spotless), the -tion ending (invitation), and an irregular spelling where the letters eigh spell the long a sound (neighbor).","Optional word-part check: ask what re- does in replant (plant it again) and what -less does in spotless (without a spot)."]}]},{id:2,title:"Equal Rows",short:"Equal Rows",parts:[{std:["3.NF.A.1"],dur:135,dir:"Look at the pictures. Answer out loud. Tell how you know.",desc:"understanding a unit fraction as one of b equal parts and a/b as a parts of that size",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The crew splits beds into rows. They split snack trays into slices. That way everyone gets the same amount.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:26px;align-items:flex-end;flex-wrap:wrap">${group(F.fracBar(4,3,{width:300}),"Bed A, 4 equal rows, 3 planted")}${group(F.fracCircle(6,5,170),"Tray B, 6 equal slices, 5 taken")}${group(stripRect([1,2,4],"A bed cut into 3 parts that are not the same size"),"Bed C, cut into 3 parts")}</div>`,qs:["Bed A is cut into 4 equal rows. What fraction of the whole bed is one row?","How much of Bed A is planted? Say the fraction. Then tell me what the top number counts.","Tray B is cut into 6 equal slices. What fraction of the tray is one slice? What fraction has been taken?","What does the bottom number of a fraction tell you?","Bed C is cut into 3 parts. Can you call the middle part 1/3 of Bed C? Tell why or why not.","A different bed is cut into 8 equal rows. Name the fraction for one row. Then name the fraction for 7 rows."],key:["1/4. The whole bed is cut into 4 equal parts, so one part is 1 of those 4 equal parts. Partial: 4 given with no fraction form is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)","3/4. The top number counts how many parts you have, and each part is 1/4, so 3/4 is 3 parts of size 1/4. Partial: the fraction right with no account of what the top number counts is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)","One slice is 1/6. The part taken is 5/6, which is 5 parts of size 1/6. Partial: one of the two fractions given is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)","It tells how many equal parts the whole is cut into. The whole has to be cut into parts of the same size. Partial: naming the number of parts with nothing said about them being the same size is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)","No. The 3 parts of Bed C are different sizes, and a third has to be 1 of 3 equal parts of the whole. Partial: saying no with nothing said about equal parts is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)","One row is 1/8. Seven rows are 7/8, which is 7 parts of size 1/8. Partial: one of the two fractions given is approaching. (3.NF.A.1)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Read the Thursday Crew",short:"Read Aloud",parts:[{std:["RF.3.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:175,dir:"Read the story out loud. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level story read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(FLU,FLU_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute and mark errors and self-corrections on your own copy. Let the student finish the passage.","Winter grade 3 benchmark: about 97 words correct per minute (Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017, winter 50th percentile). 25th percentile is about 79. The spring column rises to 112, so the bar moves at the next check, not this one. The passage length is set from the highest grade 3 figure on that same table, the spring 90th percentile of about 166 words correct per minute, so the minute ends before the text does even for the fastest reader and the score measures the reading rather than the length of the page.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for the three-part list in the sentence about pulling weeds, and for the short sentences at the end."]}]},{id:4,title:"The Share Shelf",short:"Share Shelf",parts:[{std:["RI.3.1"],dur:125,dir:"Read the text. Then answer out loud. Look back at the text to prove each answer.",desc:"questions about an informational text answered with the exact words in the text",html:()=>shelfHtml(true),qs:["What is the rule painted on the door? Read the words from the text.","How many shelves does the cabinet have? Read the words that tell you.","How much food came in during that one week in August? Find the words in the text.","How did the share shelf get started? Read the sentence that tells you.","Your turn to be the teacher. Ask one question that this text answers. Start with How, What, or Why."],key:["Take what you need, leave the rest. Reads “The rule is painted on the door: take what you need, leave the rest.” (RI.3.1)","Four. Reads “a wooden cabinet with four shelves.” (RI.3.1)","212 pounds. Reads “The total was 212 pounds of food, mostly tomatoes, squash, and beans.” (RI.3.1)","Reads “In March, a neighbor left a paper bag of extra seed packets on the garden wall with a note that said help yourself.” (RI.3.1)","Asks a question the text can answer, such as: What is the share shelf? How many families use it? Why did the crew build a cabinet? When is the shelf open? (RI.3.1, asks questions)"]}]},{id:5,title:"The Watering Path",short:"Path Lines",parts:[{std:["3.NF.A.2"],dur:140,dir:"Look at the paths. Answer out loud. Tell how you know.",desc:"naming a fraction as a number on a number line and placing fractions on a number line",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Each path runs from the gate to the far fence. The 0 is the gate. The 1 is the fence. The dot is where one watering can was set down.</p>`,fig:()=>stackFig(group(F.numberLine({min:0,max:1,step:1/3,hideNumbers:true,ticks:[[0,"0"],[1,"1"]],marks:[2/3],width:560}),"Path A, cut into 3 equal parts")+group(F.numberLine({min:0,max:1,step:.125,hideNumbers:true,ticks:[[0,"0"],[1,"1"]],marks:[.125],width:560}),"Path B, cut into 8 equal parts")+group(F.numberLine({min:0,max:2,step:1/3,labelEvery:1,marks:[4/3],width:560}),"Path C, cut into thirds")),qs:["Path A is cut into 3 equal parts. What fraction of the way along is the can?","Path B is cut into 8 equal parts. Name the fraction where the can sits. What is one part of this path called?","A path runs from 0 to 1. It is cut into 6 equal parts. How many jumps of 1/6 does it take? You get from 0 to 5/6.","Path C goes from 0 to 2 and is cut into thirds. Name the fraction where the can sits.","Tell your teacher how to draw a number line from 0 to 1 and mark 3/4 on it."],key:["2/3. The space from 0 to 1 is cut into 3 equal parts and the can sits at the end of the second part. Partial: 2/3 right with no account of the equal parts is approaching. (3.NF.A.2)","1/8. One part of this path is 1/8, because the whole from 0 to 1 is cut into 8 equal parts. Partial: the fraction right with no name for one part is approaching. (3.NF.A.2)","5 jumps. Each jump is 1/6 of the way, so 5 jumps land on 5/6. Partial: 5 given with no account of the size of a jump is approaching. (3.NF.A.2)","4/3. Accept 1 1/3. Counting thirds from 0 gives 1/3, 2/3, 3/3, 4/3, and the can is on the fourth one. Partial: 2/3 treats the whole 0 to 2 line as one whole and does not count. (3.NF.A.2)","Cut the space from 0 to 1 into 4 equal parts, then count 3 parts from 0 and mark that point. The mark goes at the end of the third part, not at the third tick counting the 0. Partial: counting ticks from the 0 instead of counting parts is the slip to watch and is approaching. (3.NF.A.2)"]}]},{id:6,title:"The Row Nobody Planted",short:"Bed Seven",parts:[{std:["RL.3.1"],dur:125,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Look back at the story to prove each answer.",desc:"asking and answering story questions using the exact words in the text",html:()=>F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true),qs:["Why did bed seven stay empty all spring? Read the words from the story.","Who noticed the empty bed first? Read the sentence that tells you.","What did the crew plant in bed seven? Find the words in the story.","What did Mr. Okafor do when he came back in June? Read the words that tell you.","Your turn to be the teacher. Ask one question that this story answers. Start with Who, What, or Why."],key:["Reads “It belonged to Mr. Okafor, who had broken his wrist in March and could not dig.” (RL.3.1)","Dalia. Reads “Dalia noticed it first.” (RL.3.1)","Reads “planted bed seven with lettuce, radishes, and two rows of beans.” (RL.3.1)","Reads “He stood at bed seven for a long minute. Then he picked one radish, held it up, and asked the crew who to thank.” (RL.3.1)","Asks a question the story can answer, such as: Who owns bed seven? What month did the crew plant it? Why could Mr. Okafor not dig? (RL.3.1, asks questions)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Which Tray Holds More?",short:"Compare",parts:[{std:["3.NF.A.3"],dur:150,dir:"Answer out loud. Tell how you know. You can draw on your paper to show your thinking.",desc:"explaining equivalent fractions and comparing fractions by reasoning about their size",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The crew writes down how full each snack tray is as a fraction. Bar A and Bar B below are the same size. So they stand for two trays that are the same size.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:28px;align-items:flex-end">${group(F.fracBar(2,1,{width:300}),"Bar A")}${group(F.fracBar(6,3,{width:300}),"Bar B")}</div>`,qs:["Which is more food on the same tray, 3/4 or 3/8? How do you know?","Which is more, 5/6 of a tray or 2/6 of the same tray? How do you know?","Look at Bar A and Bar B. Are the shaded parts equal? Say it as two fractions that are equal.","Name a fraction that is equal to 2/4.","Write 1 whole as a fraction with 5 as the bottom number. Then write the whole number 3 as a fraction.","Two trays are not the same size. Theo says 1/2 of the small tray holds more than 1/2 of the big tray. Can you compare those two halves? Tell why or why not."],key:["3/4. The top numbers are the same, so compare the piece sizes: fourths are bigger pieces than eighths, so 3 fourths is more than 3 eighths. Partial: naming 3/4 with no reason about the size of the pieces is approaching. (3.NF.A.3)","5/6. The pieces are the same size, so the one with more pieces is more. Partial: naming 5/6 with no reason is approaching. (3.NF.A.3)","Yes, they are equal. 1/2 = 3/6. The bars are the same size and the shaded parts cover the same amount. Partial: saying yes with no pair of equal fractions written is approaching. (3.NF.A.3)","1/2. Also correct: 3/6, or 4/8. Partial: 2/4 given back does not count, since a different name is what is asked. (3.NF.A.3)","5/5 and 3/1. Partial: one of the two given is approaching. (3.NF.A.3)","No. Fractions can only be compared when they name parts of the same whole. The trays are different sizes, so 1/2 of one is not the same amount as 1/2 of the other, and the fractions alone cannot settle it. Partial: saying no with nothing said about the same whole is approaching. (3.NF.A.3)"]}]},{id:8,title:"A Kitchen Like a Beehive",short:"Beehive",parts:[{std:["RL.3.4"],dur:125,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Use the story to explain each answer.",desc:"word and phrase meanings in a story, telling literal from nonliteral language",html:()=>F.passage(TABLE,TABLE_TITLE,true),qs:["The story says Mina's kitchen was a beehive all morning. Was it really full of bees? What does it mean?","Mr. Haddad is called the kind of neighbor who would give you the shirt off his back. Does that mean he hands people his shirt? What does it really tell you about him?","Mina's little brother took the job to heart. What does that mean?","What does in charge of mean in “Mina's little brother was in charge of the napkins”?","One of these means exactly what the words say and one does not. Which is which? A: “He counted them twice.” B: “The whole afternoon flew by.”"],key:["Nonliteral. It means the kitchen was busy and full of people moving around, the way a beehive is. The story shows it: her uncle chopped, her cousin stirred, and somebody kept moving the salt. (RL.3.4, nonliteral language)","Nonliteral. It means he is very generous and gives away whatever he has. The story shows it: he was already at the table with bread for everyone. (RL.3.4, nonliteral language)","Nonliteral. It means he took the job seriously and cared about doing it right. The story shows it: he counted the napkins twice. (RL.3.4, nonliteral language)","It means the napkins were his job, and he was the one responsible for them. (RL.3.4, meaning of a phrase as used in the text)","A is literal, because he really did count them two times. B is nonliteral, because an afternoon cannot fly. It means the afternoon passed very quickly. (RL.3.4, literal and nonliteral)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Split the Plots",short:"Plots",parts:[{std:["3.G.A.2"],dur:150,dir:"Look at the plots. Answer out loud. Tell how you know.",desc:"cutting shapes into parts with equal areas and naming one part as a unit fraction of the whole",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">A new plot gets split so that every helper has the same amount of ground to dig.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:24px;align-items:flex-end;flex-wrap:wrap">${group(F.gridRect(2,3,0,{cell:72}),"Plot A")}${group(stripRect([1,1,1,1],"A square cut into 4 equal strips"),"Plot B")}${group(F.fracCircle(8,0,180),"Plot C")}${group(stripRect([1,2,4],"A square cut into 3 strips that are not the same width"),"Plot D")}</div>`,qs:["Plot A is cut into 6 parts with equal areas. What fraction of the whole plot is one part?","Plot B is cut into equal strips. What fraction of the whole plot is one strip?","Plot C is a round plot cut into equal parts. What fraction of the whole plot is one part?","Plot D is cut into 3 parts. Can you call one of those parts 1/3 of the plot? Tell why or why not.","A whole plot has an area of 12 square feet and is cut into 4 parts with equal areas. What fraction of the plot is one part. What is the area of one part?","Tell your teacher two different ways to cut a square plot into 3 parts with equal areas. What fraction of the square is each part?"],key:["1/6. The plot is cut into 6 parts with the same area, so one part is 1 of 6 equal parts of the whole. Partial: 6 given with no fraction form is approaching. (3.G.A.2)","1/4. There are 4 equal strips, so one strip is 1/4 of the plot. Partial: 4 given with no fraction form is approaching. (3.G.A.2)","1/8. The round plot is cut into 8 equal parts, so one part is 1/8 of it. Partial: 8 given with no fraction form is approaching. (3.G.A.2)","No. The 3 strips do not have the same area, and a third has to be 1 of 3 parts with equal areas. Partial: saying no with nothing said about equal areas is approaching. (3.G.A.2)","1/4 of the plot, and 3 square feet. 12 square feet split into 4 equal parts gives 3 square feet for each part, because 4 × 3 = 12. Partial: one of the fraction and the area given is approaching. (3.G.A.2)","Any two correct ways, such as 3 strips side by side going down, 3 rows across, or a 3 by 3 grid of 9 small squares taken 3 at a time. Each part is 1/3 of the square. Partial: one correct way given is approaching. (3.G.A.2)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Fix the Gate Notice",short:"Fix It",parts:[{title:"One Mistake Each",std:["L.3.1"],dur:80,dir:"Each line from the garden notice has one mistake. Say the whole line out loud the right way.",desc:"fixing spoken grammar mistakes (plurals, verb tense, agreement, comparing words, pronouns)",qs:["The womans carried soup down to the long table.","Last week we bringed tomatoes to the share shelf.","Two helpers was raking the path by the gate.","That is the most kindest note on the whole board.","Priya finished her row, and then she put away his rake."],key:["The women carried soup down to the long table. (L.3.1, irregular plural noun)","Last week we brought tomatoes to the share shelf. (L.3.1, irregular past tense verb)","Two helpers were raking the path by the gate. (L.3.1, subject-verb agreement)","That is the kindest note on the whole board. (L.3.1, superlative adjective)","Priya finished her row, and then she put away her rake. (L.3.1, pronoun that matches its noun)"]},{title:"Build a Line",std:["L.3.1"],dur:60,dir:"Answer out loud. Say whole sentences.",desc:"spoken grammar items (joining sentences, future tense, adverbs, abstract nouns)",qs:["Join these two sentences into one. Use because: Theo brought an extra pair of gloves. His friend had cold hands.","Change this sentence so it tells about tomorrow: The crew waters the beds and pulls the weeds.","Which word fits? The gate closed ____ behind them. Is it soft or softly?","Which word names something you cannot see or touch: rake, carrot, or kindness?"],key:["Theo brought an extra pair of gloves because his friend had cold hands. (L.3.1, subordinating conjunction that joins a cause to a result)","The crew will water the beds and will pull the weeds. Both verbs need to change, so accept “will water the beds and pull the weeds” too. (L.3.1, simple future tense)","softly (L.3.1, adverb that tells how)","kindness (L.3.1, abstract noun)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Listen to the Plan",short:"Listen",parts:[{std:["SL.3.2"],n:5,dur:130,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads. Then answer out loud. For the last two, use the graph.",desc:"main ideas and supporting details from a text read aloud and from a graph",read:PLAN_READ,html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Your teacher will read you a short report about the Saturday work morning. Listen for the big idea and for the details that back it up. Then look at the graph.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.barGraph({title:"Helpers who signed up",cats:["Watering","Weeding","Picking"],vals:[6,10,8],max:12,step:2}),"Last Saturday's job sign-up"]),qs:["What is the main idea of the report your teacher read?","Give two details from the report that back up that main idea.","How long does the Saturday morning take? Which part of the report told you?","Look at the graph. What is the main idea the graph shows?","Give one supporting detail from the graph."],key:["Main idea: the crew gets everything ready on Friday night and posts a job list, so on Saturday nobody has to wait around or ask what to do. (SL.3.2, main idea of a text read aloud)","Any two details: two people write the job list on Friday and hang it on the gate; one person fills the water barrels; one person counts the trowels because more people come than sign up; the tools are out and the list is up by eight; new helpers pick a job off the list. (SL.3.2, supporting details)","About two hours. It came from the last part of the report. (SL.3.2, supporting detail)","The three jobs do not draw the same number of helpers. Weeding draws the most. (SL.3.2, main idea presented visually and quantitatively)","Any detail read correctly from the graph: weeding had 10 helpers, picking had 8, watering had 6, watering had the fewest, or 4 more people signed up to weed than to water. (SL.3.2, supporting detail presented quantitatively)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Tell Me About It",short:"Tell Me",parts:[{std:["SL.3.4"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:110,dir:"Talk for about one minute. Speak clearly and not too fast.",desc:"reporting on a topic with facts and descriptive details, speaking clearly",html:()=>`<p class="big">Tell me about a time someone helped you, or a time you helped someone. Say what happened, and add details so I can picture it.</p>${pills("What happened","Facts","Details")}`,key:["Gives facts that fit the topic, told in an order a listener can follow (SL.3.4)","Adds relevant, descriptive details, such as what it looked, sounded, or felt like (SL.3.4)","Speaks clearly at a pace you can follow (SL.3.4)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Shelf Notes",short:"Notes",parts:[{std:["W.3.8"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:100,dir:"Read the text. Write your notes on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"taking brief notes from a text and sorting them into given categories",html:()=>`${shelfHtml(true)}<p class="big">Make two boxes on your paper. Label them like the boxes below. Write two short notes from the text in each box.</p>${noteBoxes("What the share shelf is","How the shelf got started")}<p class="small" style="text-align:center;font-size:1.2rem">A note is just a few words. You do not need a whole sentence.</p>`,key:["What it is box: two facts, such as a wooden cabinet with four shelves, behind the garden gate, rule says take what you need and leave the rest, open seven in the morning until dark, about 38 families use it, 212 pounds in one week. (W.3.8, sorts evidence into the given category)","How it started box: two facts, such as a neighbor left a bag of seed packets in March, note said help yourself, three bags by the end of the week, five days of rain wrecked the bags, crew built the cabinet in April. (W.3.8, sorts evidence into the given category)","Notes are brief: a few words or a short phrase, not a whole sentence copied out of the text. (W.3.8, brief notes)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Garden Writer",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"opinion",label:"Opinion",icon:"💬",hint:"Second shelf or a tool cart?"},std:["W.3.1"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:200,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Westmill garden has saved enough for one new thing this year. It can build a second share shelf next to the school, or it can buy a rolling tool cart with eight sets of gloves and trowels. A second shelf would put food closer to the families who live by the school. A tool cart would mean nobody has to wait for a trowel on a work morning.</p><p class="big">Which should the garden pick? Write your opinion and give reasons. Write 4 or 5 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Say your opinion in the first sentence","Give two reasons for it","Use a linking word: because, since, also, for example","End with a closing sentence"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic and states a clear opinion about the second shelf or the tool cart (W.3.1)","Gives reasons that support the opinion, such as food closer to families by the school, or nobody waiting for a trowel (W.3.1)","Uses linking words such as because, since, also, for example, therefore (W.3.1)","Ends with a concluding sentence (W.3.1)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"facts",label:"Explain",icon:"📘",hint:"Teach about the share shelf"},std:["W.3.2"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:200,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write to teach someone how the share shelf works and how it got started. Use facts from the text. Write 5 or 6 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Start with a sentence that names your topic","Give facts from the text, and add a detail to at least one of them","Use linking words: also, another, because","End with a closing sentence"])}${lookBack("the text",shelfHtml(true))}`,key:["Introduces the topic: what the share shelf is and how it works (W.3.2)","Develops the topic with facts from the text, such as the cabinet has four shelves, the rule is take what you need and leave the rest, it is open seven in the morning until dark, 212 pounds came in one week, about 38 families use it, it began with one bag of seed packets in March, and adds at least one detail (W.3.2)","Uses linking words such as also, another, and, because (W.3.2)","Ends with a concluding sentence (W.3.2)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Two Share Shelves",short:"Two Shelves",parts:[{std:["RI.3.9"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Read the second text. Then answer out loud about both texts.",desc:"comparing and contrasting the most important points and key details in two texts on the same topic",html:()=>`${pantryHtml()}${lookBack("the share shelf text",shelfHtml(true))}`,qs:["Both texts are about a free shelf anyone can use. What is the most important point they both make?","Name one way the Westmill shelf and the Grove Road box are different.","Which text tells you more about how the shelf got started? How do you know?"],key:["Both say take what you need, and both are open to anyone with nothing to sign and nothing to pay. Either half counts","Any real difference: a wooden cabinet behind a garden gate against a metal box bolted to a post at a bus stop, fresh garden food against cans and rice, open until dark against a light inside so it works after dark, a known start against nobody remembering who started it","The Westmill text. It tells the whole start: a neighbor left a bag of seed packets in March with a note saying help yourself, three bags by the end of the week, rain wrecked them, and the crew built the cabinet in April. The Grove Road text says nobody remembers who put the first can in"]}]},{id:16,title:"Trays and Shares",short:"Groups",parts:[{title:"Groups of Pots",std:["3.OA.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud. Write the last answer on your paper.",desc:"interpreting a product as a number of equal groups",qs:["The crew sets out 6 trays with 7 pots in each tray. What does 6 x 7 mean here? How many pots in all?","In 8 x 5, if the 8 is the number of rows, what does the 5 count?","Write a multiplication equation for 4 rows with 9 plants in each row."],key:["6 groups of 7, that is 6 trays with 7 pots in each. 42 pots in all. Partial: 42 given with no account of what 6 x 7 means is approaching.","How many plants are in each row. Partial: naming the rows again instead of what is in each row does not count.","4 x 9 = 36. Or: 9 x 4 = 36 counts when the student says 9 columns of 4."]},{title:"Equal Shares",std:["3.OA.A.2"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud. Write the last answer on your paper.",desc:"interpreting a quotient as the size of an equal share or the number of shares",qs:["56 carrots are shared equally into 8 bags. What does 56 divided by 8 mean here? How many carrots in each bag?","42 seedlings are packed into trays that hold 6 each. How many trays?","Write a division equation for 45 bulbs split into 9 equal piles."],key:["It means 56 split into 8 equal shares, so it asks how many are in one bag. 7 carrots in each bag. Partial: 7 given with no account of what the division means is approaching.","7 trays. Here the division asks how many groups of 6 fit in 42. Partial: 7 given with no account of what the division asks is approaching.","45 divided by 9 = 5. Writing it as 45 / 9 = 5 is fine. Partial: 9 divided by 45 reverses the division and does not count."]}]},{id:17,title:"Two Steps at the Gate",short:"Two Steps",parts:[{std:["3.OA.D.8"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Solve on your paper. Write an equation with a letter for the unknown. Say your answer.",desc:"two-step word problems with an equation using a letter for the unknown, and checking for reasonableness",qs:["The crew picked 124 tomatoes. They left 38 on the share shelf and split the rest equally between 2 boxes. How many tomatoes went in each box? Write an equation with a letter.","A bag of seed packets costs 4 dollars. The crew buys 7 bags and pays with a 50 dollar bill. How much change do they get?","Use rounding to check your change answer. Is it reasonable?"],key:["43 tomatoes. Two steps: 124 - 38 = 86, then 86 divided by 2 = 43. The equation needs a letter, such as t = (124 - 38) / 2. Partial: 86 stops before the split between the two boxes and is approaching.","22 dollars. 7 x 4 = 28, then 50 - 28 = 22. Partial: 28 stops at the cost and leaves the change out, so it is approaching.","Yes. 7 bags at about 4 dollars is about 30 dollars, and 50 - 30 = 20, which is close to 22. Look for an estimate, not a second exact answer. Partial: a second exact answer instead of an estimate does not count."]}]},{id:18,title:"Round the Shelf Numbers",short:"Rounding",parts:[{std:["3.NBT.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"rounding whole numbers to the nearest ten and the nearest hundred",qs:["The share shelf took in 212 pounds of food in one week. Round 212 to the nearest ten. Now round it to the nearest hundred.","About 38 families use the shelf. Round 38 to the nearest ten.","Round 465 to the nearest hundred. How did you decide?"],key:["210 to the nearest ten, because 212 is nearer to 210 than to 220. 200 to the nearest hundred, because 212 is nearer to 200 than to 300. Partial: one of the two roundings right is approaching.","40, because 38 is nearer to 40 than to 30. Partial: 30 rounds down when 38 is nearer 40 and does not count.","500. 465 is between 400 and 500, and 465 is past the halfway number 450, so it rounds up. Partial: 400 rounds down when 465 sits past 450 and does not count."]}]},{id:19,title:"Area of a Bed",short:"Area",parts:[{std:["3.MD.C.7"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look at the bed. Answer out loud.",desc:"relating area to multiplication and to addition over two side by side rectangles",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">A raised bed is marked off in square feet.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row(F.gridRect(4,6,0,{cell:40})),qs:["How many rows does this bed have. How many squares are in each row? What is the area?","A second bed is 3 squares by 7 squares. What is its area?","Two beds sit side by side. Both are 5 squares deep. One is 6 squares wide and the other is 4 squares wide. What is the total area? Show it as one multiplication."],key:["4 rows of 6, so the area is 4 x 6 = 24 square units. Counting all 24 squares is correct, but the multiplication is what to look for. Partial: counting all 24 squares counts here, and the multiplication is the thing to note.","21 square units (3 x 7 = 21). Partial: 20 from adding the sides gives the perimeter and does not count.","50 square units. 5 x 6 = 30 and 5 x 4 = 20, and 30 + 20 = 50. As one multiplication it is 5 x (6 + 4) = 5 x 10 = 50. Partial: the two beds right with no single multiplication written is approaching."]}]},{id:20,title:"Measure the Seedlings",short:"Line Plot",parts:[{std:["3.MD.B.4"],n:4,dur:115,dir:"Look at the ruler and the line plot. Answer out loud. For the last one, get a ruler and measure.",mats:["a ruler"],desc:"measuring to the half and quarter inch, generating measurements of real objects with a ruler, and reading a line plot of the measurements",fig:()=>F.row(F.ruler({length:6,item:3.25,itemLabel:"seedling",parts:4,unit:"inches"})),fig2:()=>F.row(dotPlot()),qs:["How tall is the seedling on the ruler? Use halves or quarters of an inch.","The crew measured 8 seedlings and made this line plot. How many were 3 and a quarter inches tall?","How many seedlings were taller than 3 inches?","Now you are the measurer. Find three small things at your table. Something like a pencil. Or an eraser. Or a paper clip. Measure each one with your ruler. Say what each thing is. Say how long it is, to the nearest quarter inch."],key:["3 and 1/4 inches. The seedling starts at 0 and ends one tick past the 3, and each small tick is a quarter inch. Partial: 3 inches rounds to a whole and is approaching.","3 seedlings. Partial: counting the dots above a different mark does not count.","4 seedlings. Three at 3 and 1/4 and one at 3 and 1/2. A student who counts the 3 inch dot as well will say 5, so ask what taller than means. Partial: 5 counts the 3 inch dot as well, so it does not count.","Three objects of the student's own choosing, each measured and named with a length. Watch the 0 end of the ruler line up with the end of the object, and listen for a length to the nearest quarter inch (a whole, 1/4, 1/2, or 3/4) with the unit inches said. Write the three objects and lengths on the record sheet, and have the student hold one object on the ruler up to the camera so you can check that reading yourself. Or: any three objects count, since these are the student's own data and there is no fixed answer. Partial: three lengths given but all rounded to whole inches, or the ruler started at the 1 instead of the 0, is approaching."]}]},{id:21,title:"A Day at the Garden",short:"Story",parts:[{std:["W.3.3"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:70,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a short narrative with a situation, events in order, details, and a closing",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write a short story about a day at a garden like Westmill. It can be real or made up. Tell who is there, what happens, and how it ends. Write 4 or 5 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Start by telling who is there and where they are","Tell what happens, in order","Use at least one detail you can see, hear, or smell","End the story"])}`,key:["Sets up the situation and introduces at least one person or narrator (W.3.3)","Events happen in an order that makes sense, with a time word such as then, after that, or later (W.3.3)","Uses at least one descriptive detail or a line of dialogue (W.3.3)","Ends with a sense of closure, not a sentence that stops in the middle (W.3.3)","Four or five sentences is the whole job here. Do not ask for more."]}]},{id:22,title:"Listening at the Library",short:"Senses",parts:[{title:"Sense, Brain, Act",std:["3-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. For the last one, draw your model on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"using a model to show how an animal takes in information, processes it, and responds",html:()=>`<p class="big">A gentle old dog visits the library. Children can read to him there. He lies with his chin on the carpet. A child starts reading in a soft voice. His ears turn toward her. He shifts over until he is leaning against her knee.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("dog",96)}${F.icon("ear",80)}${F.icon("books",88)}</div>`,qs:["Which body part took in the information about the child’s voice?","He did not move toward the loud shelving cart. That cart was on the other side of the room. He moved toward her. What had to happen inside him before he chose?","Draw and label a three-stage model of what happened, with an arrow between the stages."],key:["His ears, which turned toward her voice. An ear is a sense organ for sound. (3-LS1-1)","His brain compared the sounds coming in and decided which one to move toward. The deciding happens in the brain, not in the ear. (3-LS1-1)","Three parts in order with arrows: ears or hears the voice, then brain, then leans against her knee. The order is what is rated. A model with nothing in the middle is incomplete. (3-LS1-1)"]},{title:"Taking Turns on Watch",std:["3-LS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"building an argument from data that forming a group helps animals survive",html:()=>`<p class="big">Meerkats feed in the open. In a group, one animal stands up on watch while the rest dig. A team recorded how the feeding went.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Meerkats</th><th>Minutes spent digging, out of 30</th><th>Hawk attacks that caught someone</th></tr>
     <tr><td>one meerkat on its own</td><td>11</td><td>4</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a group of nine with one on watch</td><td>26</td><td>0</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which meerkats got more digging done in half an hour?","Make an argument that living in a group helps a meerkat survive. Use a number from the table.","The animal on watch is not eating at all. Why is that still a good deal for it?"],key:["the group of nine, 26 minutes out of 30 against 11 (3-LS2-1)","The claim yes, with the numbers attached: a meerkat in the group dug for 26 minutes instead of 11 and nobody was caught, against four catches for the one alone, so it gets more food and is safer. A yes with no number is not yet an argument. (3-LS2-1)","Because every animal takes a turn, so it loses a little feeding time once and is watched over by somebody else the rest of the day. The trade is worth it to each one. (3-LS2-1)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Kits and Bean Plants",short:"Traits",parts:[{title:"Passed Down and Shaped",std:["3-LS3-1","3-LS3-2"],n:4,dur:120,dir:"Look at both tables. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading trait data for parents and offspring, naming the variation, and telling when the surroundings shaped a trait",html:()=>`<p class="big">Table 1: a pair of rabbits with a white patch on the forehead, and their five kits.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Rabbit</th><th>Forehead</th><th>Ear length in cm</th></tr>
     <tr><td>parent 1</td><td>white patch</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>parent 2</td><td>white patch</td><td>12</td></tr>
     <tr><td>kit A</td><td>white patch</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>kit B</td><td>white patch</td><td>12</td></tr>
     <tr><td>kit C</td><td>white patch</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>kit D</td><td>white patch</td><td>13</td></tr>
     <tr><td>kit E</td><td>white patch</td><td>10</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Table 2: two bean plants grown from seeds out of the same pod.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Plant</th><th>How it was grown</th><th>Height</th><th>Pods</th></tr>
     <tr><td>plant 1</td><td>tied to a tall stake</td><td>160 cm, upright</td><td>22</td></tr>
     <tr><td>plant 2</td><td>left to sprawl on the ground</td><td>50 cm tall, vines spread wide</td><td>7</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Every kit has a white forehead patch, the same as both parents. Where did that trait come from?","The ear lengths are all a little different. What is that difference called. Does it mean one of them is not theirs?","In Table 2 both seeds came out of the same pod. What made one upright with 22 pods and the other sprawling with 7?","So is a bean plant’s shape decided only by the seed? Explain."],key:["It was inherited from the parents. A trait both parents have and every offspring has is passed down. (3-LS3-1)","Variation. They are all still their kits: inherited traits vary from one offspring to the next, so a group of similar organisms is not identical. (3-LS3-1)","Whether it had a stake to climb. Same pod, different surroundings, different plant. (3-LS3-2)","No. The environment shaped it too. A plant that can climb puts its growth into height and pods, and one on the ground spreads out instead. Listen for the environment named as the cause. (3-LS3-2)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Stone and Survival",short:"Fossils",parts:[{title:"Clues in the Creek Bank",std:["3-LS4-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table of rock layers. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading fossil data to say which organisms lived in a place and what the place was like",html:()=>`<p class="big">A high flood tore a new face into the creek bank behind the garden and exposed three layers. A class listed the fossils. The top layer is the youngest.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Fossils found</th></tr>
     <tr><td>top</td><td>willow leaf prints, a dragonfly wing</td></tr>
     <tr><td>middle</td><td>fern fronds, a salamander bone</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bottom</td><td>sea snail shells, a sea urchin spine</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("snail",80)}${F.icon("leaf",80)}${F.icon("butterfly",80)}</div>`,qs:["Name two organisms that once lived where this creek bank now stands.","Was the bottom layer salt water or fresh water? Use the fossils as your evidence.","Both the middle and the top layers hold living things that like damp ground. What does that suggest about this place through that whole stretch of time?"],key:["Any two the table names: a willow, a dragonfly, a fern, a salamander, a sea snail, a sea urchin. (3-LS4-1)","Salt water, the sea. Sea snails and sea urchins live in the ocean, and naming them is the evidence. An answer with no fossil named is not yet using the data. (3-LS4-1)","That it stayed wet or near water for a long time, even after the sea was gone. Ferns, salamanders, willows and dragonflies all need damp ground or fresh water. (3-LS4-1)"]},{title:"A Useful Difference",std:["3-LS4-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining how a variation among individuals of one species gives an advantage",html:()=>`<p class="big">The garden is planted with deep tube-shaped flowers. A class measured bumblebee tongues and watched how many flowers each bee could feed from.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Bees</th><th>Tongue length</th><th>Flowers fed from in ten minutes</th><th>Nectar brought back</th></tr>
     <tr><td>short tongues</td><td>7 mm</td><td>4</td><td>a little</td></tr>
     <tr><td>long tongues</td><td>14 mm</td><td>19</td><td>a full load</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which bees fed from more flowers?","Explain why a long tongue is an advantage in a garden of deep tube-shaped flowers. Use the table.","The long-tongued bees also raised more young in the nest. How can a trait that helps a bee feed end up giving it more young?"],key:["the long-tongued bees, 19 flowers against 4 (3-LS4-2)","A long tongue reaches nectar at the bottom of a deep tube, so those bees can feed from flowers the short-tongued bees cannot, 19 against 4. The explanation has to lean on the data and has to tie the tongue to the deep flowers. (3-LS4-2)","More nectar brought home feeds more young in the nest, so more of them grow up. Accept any version that links feeding to surviving and raising young. (3-LS4-2)"]}]},{id:25,title:"The Meadow Was Mowed",short:"Habitat",parts:[{title:"Who Survives, and What Would Help",std:["3-LS4-3","3-LS4-4"],n:4,dur:120,dir:"Read the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing from data who can survive in a changed habitat, then judging the merit of a solution",html:()=>`<p class="big">The rough meadow beside the garden used to be left tall. This year it was mowed short every two weeks all summer. A class counted before and after.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Living thing</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr>
     <tr><td>short lawn grass</td><td>patchy</td><td>thick across the whole meadow</td></tr>
     <tr><td>butterflies</td><td>37 counted in a week</td><td>6 counted in a week</td></tr>
     <tr><td>meadow voles, which nest in tall grass</td><td>14 nests found</td><td>no nests found</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which living thing survived well after the mowing? Give the evidence.","Which one could not survive there at all? which one survived less well?","The class suggests leaving a strip along the fence unmowed all summer. Would that help the voles? Name one drawback.","A second plan is to mow the whole meadow only once, in late autumn. Which plan has more merit, and why?"],key:["The short lawn grass. It went from patchy to thick across the whole meadow, so frequent mowing suited it. (3-LS4-3)","The meadow voles could not survive there at all, from 14 nests down to none. The butterflies survived less well, from 37 down to 6. (3-LS4-3)","Yes, because a tall strip gives the voles the cover they nest in. Then any real drawback: a narrow strip may be too small for many voles, it only helps along one edge, somebody has to remember not to mow it, long grass by a fence may be a fire worry in dry weather. A claim with no drawback is half of this standard. (3-LS4-4)","Either plan can earn credit. What is rated is the reasoning: mowing once in autumn gives tall grass and flowers across the whole meadow for the whole summer, which helps voles and butterflies both, but it makes the meadow harder to walk through and the autumn mowing is a bigger job, while a strip is easy and helps only a little. (3-LS4-4)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Weather and Climate",short:"Weather",parts:[{title:"A Season of Weather",std:["3-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud. For the last one, draw your graph on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"reading and making a graphical display of typical weather for one season",html:()=>`<p class="big">The garden club measured the rain in its gauge every week of late winter.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Week</th><th>Rain in mm</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>22</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>35</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>26</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("rain",88)}${F.icon("umbrella",88)}</div>`,qs:["Which week was the wettest, and which was the driest?","Is this season steady from week to week, or does it jump around? What would you tell someone to expect in a typical week?","Draw a bar graph of the five weeks. Label both sides."],key:["wettest week 3 with 35 mm, driest week 2 with 8 mm (3-ESS2-1)","It jumps around. A typical week brings about 20 to 25 mm, but any one week can be much wetter or much drier. The point is a typical value with the spread noticed. (3-ESS2-1)","Five bars in week order with heights that match 22, 8, 35, 19 and 26, a label on the week side and a label on the rain side. A graph with no labels is incomplete. (3-ESS2-1)"]},{title:"Two Parts of the World",std:["3-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"combining information to describe the climates of two different regions of the world",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two regions in the same country, one on the coast and one inland behind a mountain range.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Region</th><th>Summer average</th><th>Winter average</th><th>Rain in a year</th></tr>
     <tr><td>a coastal region</td><td>19 C</td><td>11 C</td><td>60 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>an inland desert behind the mountains</td><td>37 C</td><td>6 C</td><td>12 cm</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("wave",80)}${F.icon("cactus",80)}</div>`,qs:["Which region swings further between summer and winter, and by how much?","The inland region sits behind a mountain range. How could mountains help explain why it gets so little rain?","Give one other reason why two regions end up with different climates."],key:["the inland desert, a swing of 31 degrees from 6 C to 37 C, against 8 degrees on the coast (3-ESS2-2)","Air blowing in off the ocean has to rise over the mountains, and it drops its rain on the way up, so the air that reaches the far side is dry. Accept the idea in the student’s own words. (3-ESS2-2)","Any other sound reason: how near a region is to the middle of the globe, how near it is to an ocean, how high it sits above sea level, which way the wind usually blows. (3-ESS2-2)"]},{title:"Ready for the Water",std:["3-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"making a claim about the merit of a design that reduces the impact of a weather hazard",html:()=>`<p class="big">Heavy winter rain floods the low path through the garden. The beds beside it turn to mud for a week. The club builds a raised wooden walkway on short posts. It runs down the middle of the path. There is gravel underneath.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("rain",88)}${F.icon("sprout",88)}</div>`,qs:["Which weather hazard is the walkway meant to reduce?","Make a claim. Does the raised walkway have merit? Give your reason.","Name one thing the walkway does not fix."],key:["flooding of the path after heavy rain (3-ESS3-1)","Yes, with a reason attached: people walk above the water instead of through it, and the gravel lets the water drain away instead of standing. A yes with no reason is not yet a claim. (3-ESS3-1)","Any real limit: the beds themselves are still waterlogged, water deeper than the posts are tall, wood turning slippery when wet, it does not stop the rain reaching the plants, the posts rotting over time. (3-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Carrying Together",short:"Forces",parts:[{title:"Balanced or Not",std:["3-PS2-1"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"planning an investigation of balanced and unbalanced forces acting on an object",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two people carry a heavy garden bench, one at each end. They lift together and hold it steady in the air. The bench stays level and still.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("hands",96)}${F.icon("box",80)}</div>`,qs:["The bench is heavy and gravity pulls it down, yet it stays still in the air. Are the forces on it balanced or unbalanced? How do you know?","The person at one end lowers her hands a little. What happens to that end, and what changed about the forces?","Plan an investigation to find out. Is the bench easier to carry? Try it when the load is shared evenly. What will you change? What will you measure?","What will you keep the same every time you test?"],key:["Balanced. The two lifts together push up exactly as hard as gravity pulls down, and the bench holding still is the evidence. (3-PS2-1)","That end drops. The lift on that end is now less than the downward pull there, so the forces on that end are unbalanced. (3-PS2-1)","A plan with one thing changed and one thing measured: carry the same bench with the load even and then with one person holding nearer the middle (the change) and time the carry over a set distance, or read a scale under each pair of hands (the measure). (3-PS2-1)","Any controlled variable that fits the plan: the same bench, the same two carriers, the same distance and route, the same grip height, the same walking pace. (3-PS2-1)"]},{title:"Predict the Next Mark",std:["3-PS2-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a pattern in motion data to predict where an object will be later",html:()=>`<p class="big">A ball is rolled along the smooth hall floor, past chalk marks spaced 2 m apart. A timer calls out each second.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Seconds</th><th>Mark the ball is passing</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>6</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What pattern do these numbers make?","Which mark will the ball pass at 6 seconds. How far from the start is that in meters?","A second ball passed mark 2 at one second and mark 3 at two seconds. Would you expect it to reach mark 6 at the same time as the first ball? Explain."],key:["Two marks every second, the same number each time. The ball is moving at a steady speed. (3-PS2-2)","mark 12, which is 24 m from the start, because 12 marks at 2 m each is 24 m. Both parts have to be there. (3-PS2-2)","No. The second ball is slower, one mark a second instead of two, so it reaches mark 6 at six seconds while the first ball got there at three. The prediction comes from each ball’s own pattern. (3-PS2-2)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Pulling Without Touching",short:"Magnets",parts:[{title:"Ask About the Pull",std:["3-PS2-3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. Your two questions have to be questions, not statements.",desc:"asking questions about cause and effect in magnetic interactions between objects not touching",html:()=>`<p class="big">A paperclip is laid on a sheet of card. A magnet is moved underneath the card. The paperclip slides along on top, following it. A second magnet is brought near the first. The two push each other away with nothing between them.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("key",88)}${F.icon("pencil",80)}</div>`,qs:["The magnet never touches the paperclip, yet the clip moves. What does that tell you about this force?","What two questions would you want to investigate here?","The two magnets push each other away. What is causing that push. What would you do to make them pull together instead?"],key:["The force acts across a distance and straight through the card, with no contact between the objects. (3-PS2-3)","Any two real investigable questions: how many sheets of card can it work through, does a heavier clip still follow, does a copper wire follow, how fast can the magnet move before the clip is left behind, does the side of the magnet matter. A statement is not a question, so ask once more. (3-PS2-3)","The ends facing each other are the same kind of pole, and like poles push apart. Turning one magnet around puts opposite poles together and they pull. (3-PS2-3)"]},{title:"A Problem for a Magnet",std:["3-PS2-4","3-5-ETS1-1"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Answer out loud. For the third one you may write your two sentences on your paper.",desc:"defining a design problem that magnets can solve, with criteria for success and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Steel pins drop off the edge of the sewing table. That table is in the library. They scatter across the carpet. People sitting at the table find them. They use their hands or their feet.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("key",80)}${F.icon("box",80)}${F.icon("heart",72)}</div>`,qs:["Name the problem in one sentence. Your sentence starts: The problem is.","Why is a magnet a sensible idea here? Would the same tool pick up a dropped plastic button?","Write down two things the tool has to do to be a success.","You may use three things only. A yardstick, one magnet, and a rubber band. It has to be safe for a small child. What limits does that put on your design?"],key:["A problem statement about picking dropped steel pins out of the carpet so nobody is stuck by one. The problem has to be about the pins in the carpet, not about the sewing. (3-PS2-4)","The pins are steel, and a magnet pulls steel without touching it, so it lifts pins out of the carpet without anyone feeling for them. A plastic button is not pulled by a magnet, so no. (3-PS2-4)","Two criteria, written as what the tool must do: pick up every pin, work without anyone touching the carpet, get the pins back into the tin, be used standing up, leave no pin behind. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Names the limits as limits: only three materials, only one magnet, and the design has to be safe for a small child, which rules out sharp or loose parts. Materials, time, cost and safety are all real constraints. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Compare and Test",short:"Design",parts:[{title:"Three Ways to Do It",std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table and the three rules. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing several possible solutions against stated criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">The library door is heavy, and people carrying a stack of books cannot pull it open. Three designs are tried.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Opens with no hands?</th><th>Cost</th><th>Keeps cold air out?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>a foot pedal that swings the door</td><td>yes</td><td>60 dollars</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a doorway left permanently open</td><td>yes</td><td>nothing</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a long push bar at hip height</td><td>yes, with a hip or elbow</td><td>25 dollars</td><td>yes</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The rules for success: open without using hands, cost under 40 dollars, keep the cold air out.</p>`,qs:["Which design costs the least?","Which design meets all three rules? Explain using the table.","Name a design you would turn down. The rule that rules it out."],key:["the permanently open doorway, which costs nothing (3-5-ETS1-2)","The long push bar at hip height. It opens with a hip or elbow, costs 25 dollars, and keeps the cold out. Another choice counts only if the student argues it against all three rules using the table. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Any reasoned rejection with the rule named: the foot pedal fails the cost rule at 60 dollars, the open doorway fails the cold-air rule. Naming the rule is the part being rated. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{title:"Make the Test Fair",std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"judging whether a test is fair, naming controlled variables, and identifying a failure point",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two glues were tested to see which holds a paper card more strongly.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Glue</th><th>Amount used</th><th>Drying time before the pull</th><th>Card</th></tr>
     <tr><td>glue A</td><td>one small dot</td><td>10 minutes</td><td>thin paper</td></tr>
     <tr><td>glue B</td><td>a thick line</td><td>a whole day</td><td>thick card</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Would this test tell you which glue is better? Why or why not?","Which two things would you hold steady to make this fair?","Glue B held firm in the middle but let go at the corners every single time. Name that weak spot, and say what you would change."],key:["No. Three things were different at once, the amount, the drying time and the paper, so a stronger hold cannot be pinned on the glue. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any two controlled variables: the same amount of glue, the same drying time, the same paper, the same pull, the same room conditions. (3-5-ETS1-3)","The failure point is the corners lifting. Any improvement aimed there: put glue right into each corner, press the corners while drying, round the corners off, add a dab of glue at each corner as well as the middle. An improvement somewhere else does not count. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Maps and Places",short:"Maps",parts:[{std:["HSS-3.1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Use the map grid and the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a map grid and a table to describe physical and human features and where they sit",html:()=>`<p class="big">A map grid of the town around the library. North is the top row.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>west</th><th>middle</th><th>east</th></tr>
     <tr><th>north</th><td>${F.icon("tree",54)}<br>oak woods</td><td>${F.icon("books",54)}<br>library</td><td>${F.icon("sprout",54)}<br>garden</td></tr>
     <tr><th>middle</th><td>${F.icon("mountain",54)}<br>ridge</td><td>${F.icon("cabin",54)}<br>town hall</td><td>${F.icon("bread",54)}<br>market</td></tr>
     <tr><th>south</th><td>${F.icon("drop",54)}<br>creek</td><td>${F.icon("bridge",54)}<br>footbridge</td><td>${F.icon("bus",54)}<br>bus yard</td></tr></table>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Place</th><th>People who visit on a Saturday</th></tr>
     <tr><td>library</td><td>210</td></tr><tr><td>market</td><td>480</td></tr>
     <tr><td>garden</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>oak woods</td><td>95</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What lies directly north of the town hall?","Which two directions do you walk to get from the town hall to the creek?","Give one physical feature and one human-made feature from this map.","Which place in the table draws the most people? Think of a Saturday. Picture a graph of this table. Name one thing it would show at a glance. The map does not show that."],key:["the library (HSS-3.1)","south and west, in either order. The creek sits in the south row and the west column. Partial: one of the two directions is approaching. (HSS-3.1)","Physical: the oak woods, the ridge, the creek. Human-made: the library, the garden, the town hall, the market, the footbridge, the bus yard. One of each is what is being checked. Partial: one of the two kinds named is approaching. (HSS-3.1)","the market, 480 people. A graph would show how many people go where, so you could see at a glance that the market draws more than twice the library, while the map only shows where each place is. Partial: the market named with nothing said about what the graph shows is approaching. (HSS-3.1)"]}]},{id:31,title:"Long Ago in This Place",short:"Long Ago",parts:[{title:"The First Nations Here",std:["HSS-3.2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. Use what is on the screen and what you have learned.",desc:"describing how the American Indian nations of California taught their children and held places sacred, and how a nation is part of the state today",html:()=>`<p class="big">California nations taught their children by working beside them. They taught how to find the right plants. They taught when to gather and when to leave a patch alone. They taught the stories that carried what the nation knew. Children were also taught which places their nation held sacred. Those places were on the land. Ceremonies were held there. Nobody went there to gather or hunt. Many nations run their own schools and language programs today.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("speak",88)}${F.icon("herb",80)}</div>`,qs:["The screen says children learned by working beside the grown-ups. Name the two things they were taught that way about the plants. Then say what the stories carried.","Children were taught which places their nation held sacred. Name one thing people did at such a place and one thing they did not do there. Then say what that shows about the nation’s beliefs.","Are these nations only in the past? Name one way a nation is part of the state today."],key:["How to find the right plants, and when to gather and when to leave a patch alone. The stories carried what the nation knew. Partial: the two plant lessons named with nothing said about the stories, or the stories right with only one plant lesson, is approaching. (HSS-3.2)","Ceremonies were held there, and people did not go there to gather or to hunt. It shows that the nation’s religious beliefs were part of the land itself, so some places were set apart and treated differently from the rest. Partial: the two practices named with nothing said about what they show is approaching. (HSS-3.2)","No. Any true present-day answer: nations run their own schools and language programs, they have tribal governments, they hold cultural events, they care for land and water, members live and work across the state. Partial: saying no with no present-day example is approaching. (HSS-3.2)","Not scored, ask it anyway: which American Indian nation or nations have their homeland where this student lives? The answer is different for every family, so there is no key for it and it stays out of the rating. Tell the student the name if they do not know it, write it on the record sheet, and make it something to teach before the next check."]},{title:"A Town Over Time",std:["HSS-3.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the timeline. Answer out loud.",desc:"sequencing local historical events and describing the mark each period left on the land",html:()=>`<p class="big">A timeline for the town of Willow Ford.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1853</td><td>a store opens at the willow-lined ford across the creek</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1877</td><td>a canal is dug to carry creek water out to the dry flats</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1908</td><td>the first school and library are built on the rise above the ford</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1949</td><td>the creek is lined with concrete after a bad flood</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2012</td><td>part of the concrete is taken out and willows are replanted</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Put these three in order, earliest first: the canal, the concrete lining, the first school.","Which event changed the dry flats. What did it do to them?","Pick one period on this timeline. What mark has it left on the land? Tell me one someone could still see today."],key:["canal, first school, concrete lining. Partial: two of the three in the right order is approaching. (HSS-3.3)","the canal in 1877, which carried creek water out so the dry flats could be farmed. Partial: naming the canal with no account of what it did to the flats is approaching. (HSS-3.3)","Any period with a physical mark named: the ford that set the town’s place, the canal line across the flats, the school and library on the rise, the concrete stretch of creek, the replanted willows. Partial: naming a period with no mark on the land named is approaching. (HSS-3.3)"]}]},{id:32,title:"Rules and Work",short:"Rules",parts:[{title:"Rules, Laws and Government",std:["HSS-3.4"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"telling rules from laws and naming the basic parts and levels of United States government",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class writes its own agreement about how to treat each other. Everyone signs it. The town has a law that says a public building must have a ramp as well as steps.</p>
      <p class="big">Anne Hutchinson lived in the Massachusetts colony in the 1630s. She held meetings in her own house to talk about religion and to teach what she believed, which the colony’s leaders had forbidden her to do. She was put on trial and ordered to leave. She helped settle Rhode Island, where people were allowed to worship as they chose. That freedom is written in the First Amendment to the Constitution today.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("books",88)}${F.icon("flag",88)}</div>`,qs:["How is the class agreement different from the ramp law?","Why would a government make a law about ramps instead of leaving it to each building owner?","The United States government has three parts. Name all three jobs: making laws, carrying them out, and deciding what they mean.","Anne Hutchinson was ordered out of the colony for teaching what she believed about religion. What freedom was she standing up for. Where is that freedom written down today?"],key:["The class agreement is made by the class and applies in that class. The law is made by a government, applies to every public building in the town, and the government decides what happens when it is broken. Partial: one of the two halves described is approaching. (HSS-3.4)","So that every person can get into a public building, not only the people whose building owner chose to help. Accept any answer about protecting people’s rights or treating everyone the same. Partial: saying ramps are a good idea with no account of why it has to be a law is approaching. (HSS-3.4)","the legislative branch makes laws, the executive branch carries them out, and the judicial branch, the courts, decides what they mean. Partial: two of the three jobs named is approaching. (HSS-3.4)","The freedom to worship and to speak about her own beliefs as she chose, rather than only as the colony’s leaders allowed. It is written in the First Amendment to the Constitution. Accept freedom of religion for the first part, and the Constitution or the Bill of Rights for the second. Partial: one of the two halves answered is approaching. (HSS-3.4)"]},{title:"Choices and the Local Economy",std:["HSS-3.5"],n:4,dur:105,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"basic economic reasoning about cost, price, scarcity, and goods and services",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class holds a bake sale in the library lobby to buy books for the garden shelf. They spent money on supplies first.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Item</th><th>Amount</th></tr>
     <tr><td>flour, butter and sugar they bought</td><td>18 dollars</td></tr>
     <tr><td>loaves baked</td><td>12</td></tr>
     <tr><td>price for one loaf</td><td>4 dollars</td></tr></table>`,qs:["All twelve loaves sell. How much money comes in? How much is left after paying for the supplies?","Only six loaves are baked next time, and twenty people want one. What happens to the loaves. What do we call it when there is not enough to go around?","The class sells loaves and it also wraps them as gifts for a dollar. Which is a good and which is a service?","Name someone who works in your town. Do they make something? Or do something for people? Say which."],key:["48 dollars comes in, since 12 times 4 is 48, and 30 dollars is left after the 18 dollars of supplies. Partial: the 48 dollars right with no amount left over is approaching. (HSS-3.5)","The loaves run out, or the class could charge more for the few they have. That is scarcity: there is less than people want. Accept scarcity explained in the student’s own words. Partial: saying the loaves run out with no name for there not being enough to go around is approaching. (HSS-3.5)","A loaf is a good, something you can hold. Wrapping it as a gift is a service, work someone does for you. Partial: one of the two sorted right is approaching. (HSS-3.5)","Any real local job sorted correctly: a baker makes a good, a dentist provides a service, a farmer produces a good, a librarian provides a service. Partial: a real job named with the good or service label wrong is approaching. (HSS-3.5)"]}]}];const WC=FLU.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[2].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level story read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={4:{id:"L4",title:"Level Up: Put It Together",std:["RI.4.1"],needs:["RI.3.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use details and examples from the text.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The text never says that the share shelf runs without anyone watching it. Use details from the text to explain why that is true.</li><li>Why did the crew build a cabinet in April instead of leaving the bags on the wall? The text does not say outright. Use details to explain.</li></ol>${shelfHtml(true)}`,key:["Because nothing in it is checked or recorded. Details: anyone may open it, nobody signs anything, the rule is painted on the door instead of being told to people, and it stands open from seven in the morning until dark. The text never states this outright, so the student has to put the details together. (RI.4.1, inference backed by details and examples)","To keep the food dry and off the wall. Details: the bags sat out on the garden wall, then it rained for five days and the bags fell apart, and the crew built the cabinet the next month. A cabinet with a door and shelves holds food the way a paper bag on a wall cannot. (RI.4.1, inference with details)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Unlike Pieces",std:["4.NF.A.2"],needs:["3.NF.A.2"],n:3,dir:"Answer out loud and tell how you know. You can write on your paper.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Which is greater, 3/4 of a tray or 5/8 of the same tray?</li><li>Write &gt;, &lt;, or = to make it true: 2/5 ____ 1/2</li><li>Is 5/9 of a tray more or less than half a tray? Use half as your benchmark.</li></ol>`,key:["3/4. In eighths, 3/4 is 6/8, and 6/8 is more than 5/8. (4.NF.A.2, comparing fractions with different numerators and denominators)","2/5 &lt; 1/2. In tenths, 2/5 is 4/10 and 1/2 is 5/10, so 2/5 is less. (4.NF.A.2)","More than half. Half of 9 is 4 and a half, so 5 ninths is just over half. Accept the comparison in eighteenths: 5/9 is 10/18 and 1/2 is 9/18. (4.NF.A.2, comparing to a benchmark)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Read Between the Lines",std:["RL.4.1"],needs:["RL.3.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use details from the story.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The story never says the crew wanted to keep the planting a secret. How do you know they did? Give two details from the story.</li><li>Why do you think nobody answered when Mr. Okafor asked who to thank? Use a detail from the story.</li></ol>${F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true)}`,key:["They kept it a secret, backed by two details: “they did not tell anyone,” nobody answered when he asked who to thank, and Dalia looked at her shoes and smiled instead of speaking up. (RL.4.1, inference with details)","Because they had already decided not to tell anyone, so answering would have given it away. The detail is “they did not tell anyone,” and Dalia smiling at her shoes shows she knew and still said nothing. (RL.4.1, inference with a detail)"]},7:{id:"L7",title:"Level Up: Same Amount, New Pieces",std:["4.NF.A.1"],needs:["3.NF.A.3"],n:3,dir:"Answer out loud and tell how you know. You can draw on your paper.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Write a fraction equal to 2/3 that has 12 as the bottom number.</li><li>Explain why 3/5 and 9/15 are the same amount.</li><li>Are 4/6 and 6/9 equal? Tell how you know.</li></ol>`,key:["8/12. Multiply the top and the bottom by the same number: 2 × 4 = 8 and 3 × 4 = 12. (4.NF.A.1, equivalent fractions)","Each fifth is split into 3 smaller equal parts, which turns 5 parts into 15 and turns the 3 shaded parts into 9. Nothing is added or taken away, so 3/5 = 9/15. As an equation, 3/5 = (3 × 3)/(5 × 3) = 9/15. (4.NF.A.1, explaining why a/b equals n×a over n×b)","Yes, they are equal. 4/6 is 2/3 because 4 ÷ 2 = 2 and 6 ÷ 2 = 3. 6/9 is also 2/3 because 6 ÷ 3 = 2 and 9 ÷ 3 = 3. Written in eighteenths, both are 12/18. (4.NF.A.1)"]},10:{id:"L10",title:"Level Up: Grown-Up Grammar",std:["L.4.1"],needs:["L.3.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each answer out loud.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Which word fits? The neighbor ____ left the seed packets never gave her name. Is it <b>who</b> or <b>which</b>?</li><li>Pick the right word: There were ____ many weeds in bed four. (<b>to</b>, <b>too</b>, <b>two</b>)</li><li>Change this sentence so it tells what was happening at four o'clock yesterday: Luz waters the beans.</li></ol>`,key:["who. A relative pronoun for a person is who. which is for a thing. (L.4.1, relative pronouns)","too. too means more than enough. to is a direction word and two is the number. (L.4.1, frequently confused words)","Luz was watering the beans. (L.4.1, past progressive verb tense)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.3.1","RL.3.4","RI.3.1","RI.3.9","W.3.8","RL.4.1","RI.4.1"];const MULT_N=10;const FRAC_DENS=[1,2,3,4,5,6,8];let multSel=[0,0],fracOn={};function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function fracHtml(){return FRAC_DENS.map(d=>{const on=fracOn[d]||0;let r=`<div class="fs-row">`;for(let k=0;k<d;k++)r+=`<button class="fs-part${k<on?" on":""}" data-d="${d}" data-k="${k}">${d===1?"1 whole":"1/"+d}</button>`;return r+"</div>"}).join("")+`<p class="small">Click a piece to shade up to it. Click the last shaded piece to clear the row.</p>`}function lineHtml(){const rows=[[2,"halves"],[3,"thirds"],[4,"fourths"],[6,"sixths"],[8,"eighths"]];return`<div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px">`+rows.map(r=>`<div style="text-align:center"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0">0 to 1 in ${r[1]}</p>${F.numberLine({min:0,max:1,step:1/r[0],hideNumbers:true,ticks:[[0,"0"],[1,"1"]],width:520})}</div>`).join("")+`</div><p class="small">Count the equal spaces, not the tick marks. The 0 tick is where you start counting from.</p>`}function fcardHtml(){return F.table(["Part of a fraction","What it tells you"],[["Bottom number","how many equal parts the whole is cut into"],["Top number","how many of those parts you have"],["One part, such as 1/6","one of the equal parts the whole was cut into"],["A fraction equal to 1","the top and the bottom are the same, such as 5/5"],["A whole number as a fraction","put it over 1, such as 3/1"]])+`<p class="small">The parts always have to be equal in size. If they are not equal, the pieces have no fraction name.</p>`}const SENT_SET={opinion:"opinion",facts:"facts",story:"W.3.3","W.3.1":"opinion","W.3.2":"facts","W.3.3":"W.3.3","W.3.8":"W.3.8","SL.3.2":"SL.3.2","SL.3.4":"SL.3.4"};function sentSet(st){const ps=st&&st.parts&&st.parts.length?st.parts:st?[st]:[];for(const p of ps){const a=p.alt&&p.alt.key||p.wk||p.wt;if(a&&SENT_SET[a])return SENT_SET[a];const hit=(p.std||[]).find(c=>SENT_SET[c]);if(hit)return SENT_SET[hit]}return"opinion"}function starters(st){const k=sentSet(st);if(k==="facts")return["The share shelf is ____.","The rule is ____.","One fact is ____.","Also, ____.","Now you know ____."];if(k==="W.3.3")return["One day at the garden, ____.","First, ____.","Then, ____.","In the end, ____."];if(k==="W.3.8")return["The text says ____.","What it is: ____.","How it started: ____."];if(k==="SL.3.2")return["The big idea is ____.","One detail is ____.","Another detail is ____.","The graph shows ____."];if(k==="SL.3.4")return["I want to tell you about ____.","First, ____.","One thing I remember is ____.","It looked (or sounded, or felt) like ____.","That is why ____."];return["The garden should pick ____ because ____.","One reason is ____.","For example, ____.","That is why ____."]}function sentHtml(st){return`<ul class="starters">${starters(st).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}const TOOLS={mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:["3.OA.A.1","3.OA.A.2","3.OA.D.8","3.MD.C.7","3.G.A.2","4.NF.A.1"],html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const row=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===row&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[row,k];r()})},nline:{name:"Fraction number lines",std:["3.NF.A.1","3.NF.A.2","3.NF.A.3","4.NF.A.2"],html:lineHtml},frac:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["3.NF.A.1","3.NF.A.3","3.G.A.2","4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2"],html:fracHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll(".fs-part").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const d=+x.dataset.d,k=+x.dataset.k;fracOn[d]=fracOn[d]===k+1?0:k+1;r()})},fcard:{name:"Fraction words card",std:["3.NF.A.1","3.NF.A.2","3.NF.A.3","3.G.A.2","4.NF.A.1"],html:fcardHtml},sent:{name:"Sentence starters",std:["W.3.1","W.3.2","W.3.3","W.3.8","SL.3.2","SL.3.4"],html:sentHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"mult",std:["3.G.A.2","4.NF.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the products, so rate on whether the student knows what to multiply or divide, not on the fact itself."},{t:"nline",std:["3.NF.A.1","3.NF.A.2"],msg:"The number line tool already shows the equal spaces from 0 to 1, so the partitioning is done for the student. Rate on whether the student can name the point as a fraction and say what one space is called."},{t:"nline",std:["3.NF.A.3","4.NF.A.2"],msg:"Lined-up number lines let the student see which fraction is farther from 0, so count the comparisons as supported, not independent."},{t:"frac",std:["3.NF.A.1"],msg:"Fraction strips show the equal parts already made and label each one, so rate on whether the student can say what the top and bottom numbers count."},{t:"frac",std:["3.NF.A.3","4.NF.A.1"],msg:"Fraction strips show the piece sizes and the equal shading, so rate on whether the student can explain what the strips show rather than on the comparison itself."},{t:"frac",std:["3.G.A.2"],msg:"The strips show equal parts already made, so rate on whether the student names one part as a unit fraction of the whole."},{t:"fcard",std:["3.NF.A.1","3.NF.A.2","3.NF.A.3","3.G.A.2","4.NF.A.1"],msg:"The card says what the top and bottom numbers mean, how to write 1 as a fraction, and how to write a whole number as a fraction. Count those answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"mult",std:["3.OA.A.1","3.OA.A.2"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the product and the quotient. With it open, rate whether the student can say what the numbers count in the story, not the fact itself."},{t:"mult",std:["3.OA.D.8","3.MD.C.7"],msg:"The chart supplies the facts, so rate on choosing the right two steps, or on using rows times columns, not on the arithmetic."},{t:"sent",std:["W.3.3"],msg:"Sentence starters give the student the opening, the order words, and the ending, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.3.1","W.3.2"],msg:"Sentence starters supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.3.8"],msg:"Sentence starters give the student the note categories, so rate on the facts chosen and on how brief the notes are."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.3.2"],msg:"The frames name main idea and detail for the student, so rate on whether the content of each answer is right."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.3.4"],msg:"Sentence starters give the student the order of the talk. Rate the facts, the details, and how clearly the student speaks."},{t:"passage",std:["RL.3.1","RL.3.4","RI.3.1","RI.3.9","W.3.8","RL.4.1","RI.4.1"],msg:"If the passage was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
